  abstract={Equipped with Internet of Things (IoT) devices for improved automation and efficiency, smart homes are integral to modern smart cities. However, the extensive connectivity increases the likelihood of security threats, such as hacking, data breaches, and cyber-attacks. Machine learning approaches focused on preserving the privacy of sensitive information, like user data, can provide some level of protection. This paper focuses on the cyber security of smart homes and how PPML through federated learning and homomorphic encryption can fortify such systems. These approaches can protect sensitive user information during critical functions-anomaly detection, intrusion prevention, and device authentication-within a decentralized learning framework. The research conducted simulated attacks on standard IoT datasets to test the effectiveness of PPML, demonstrating the framework's capability to balance privacy and security. The results showed that smart homes stand to benefit the most from PPML, enabling the creation of secure, privacy-respecting, and intelligent environments for users.},
